\begin{definition}[Residual]
For $t \ge 1$ let $R_t = A_t / q \in \Z$, coprime to $q$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:At}. Algebraically
$R_t = \binom{q^{t+1}-1}{q^{t}-1} - q^{\,q^{t}-1}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The composite family $n = q^{t} p$}
\label{sec:composite}

The prime fibre already forces infinitude, but with prime solutions. The richer
question concerns composite solutions of shape
\begin{equation}
  n = q^{t} p, \qquad t \ge 1,\ p \text{ prime},\ p \neq q.
  \label{eq:comp}
\end{equation}
Analysis of \eqref{eq:main} at such $n$ leads to two gates a pair $(t,p)$ must
clear: the \emph{divisibility gate} $p \mid A_t$, and the \emph{digit-sum gate}
$s_q\big((q-1)p\big) \ge (q-1)t$.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:role}
For every prime base $q$ and $t \ge 1$, the base $q$ cannot serve as the
auxiliary prime $p \neq q$ in \eqref{eq:comp}: since $\vq(A_t) = 1$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:At}), we have $A_t = q R_t$ with $\gcd(R_t, q) = 1$, so every
candidate prime $p \mid A_t$ with $p \neq q$ divides the residual $R_t$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{conjecture}[Infinitely many composite solutions]
\label{conj:comp}
For every prime base $q$ there exist infinitely many pairs $(t, p)$ with $p$
prime, $p \neq q$, $p \mid A_t$, and $s_q((q-1)p) \ge (q-1)t$; each yields a
composite solution $n = q^{t} p$ of \eqref{eq:main}.
\end{conjecture}

The heuristic is a primitive-prime-divisor phenomenon: the residuals $R_t$ grow
super-exponentially and, in the spirit of Zsygmondy and Carmichael, should
acquire a new large prime factor for infinitely many $t$; a large prime clears
the linear digit-sum gate since $s_q((q-1)p) \sim \log p$ while $(q-1)t$ is
fixed.

\section{Algorithms and numerical data}
\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Solution enumeration.} For each $n \le N$, evaluate both sides of
\eqref{eq:main} modulo $n$: $\binom{qn}{n} \bmod n$ by a modular product and
$q^{n} \bmod n$ by fast exponentiation.

\paragraph{Valuation checker.} Compute $A_t$ exactly and extract $\vq(A_t)$ by
repeated division; the output is uniformly $1$ for $t \ge 1$, confirming
Theorem~\ref{thm:At}.

\paragraph{Residual factorization.} Factor $R_t = A_t/q$, record its prime
divisors $p \neq q$, and test the digit-sum gate for each; pairs clearing both
gates give composite solutions $n = q^t p$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}
\label{sec:future}

The prime fibre is completely understood, isolating the composite family as the
frontier, and the exact one-carry valuation of $A_t$ removes $q$ from the
candidate pool, sharpening the search to primes $p \neq q$.

\paragraph{Infinitely many composite solutions.} Conjecture~\ref{conj:comp}: the
residuals $R_t = A_t/q$ grow rapidly, and a primitive-prime-divisor phenomenon
should force a new large prime factor infinitely often, automatically clearing
the digit-sum threshold.

\paragraph{Arithmetic of $R_t$.} Writing
$R_t = \binom{q^{t+1}-1}{q^{t}-1} - q^{q^{t}-1}$, we conjecture $R_t$ is
squarefree infinitely often, and for every fixed $B$ all but finitely many $R_t$
have a prime factor exceeding $B$. As a difference of a Lucas-type binomial and a
prime power, $R_t$ should be controlled by lifting-the-exponent and cyclotomic
divisibility. Since $q \parallel A_t$ exactly, all number-theoretic content is in
$R_t$.

\paragraph{The digit-sum gate.} For fixed $q, t$, we conjecture the proportion of
primes $p \le X$ with $s_q((q-1)p) \ge (q-1)t$ tends to $1$; digit sums of
$(q-1)p$ concentrate around a value proportional to $\log p$, so a fixed linear
threshold is eventually cleared by all but a vanishing set of primes.

\paragraph{A local-to-global law.} One seeks a description, for each prime $p$, of
the exponents $t$ for which $(t,p)$ clears both gates, and a global count of
composite solutions below $X$.

